	<h2>The Mission</h2>
	<p>
		<strong>ErrorLedger is the internet's engineering analysis engine.</strong>
	</p>
	<p>
		We do not publish opinion pieces, hype, or generic news summaries. Instead, we perform rigorous systems audits on trending concepts, products, business models, and cultural strategies. 
	</p>

	<h2>Our Philosophy: Everything is a System</h2>
	<p>
		We believe that every concept can be evaluated as a system operating under real-world constraints. A viral diet is a biological system. A trading algorithm is a probabilistic system. A startup is a distributed system.
	</p> 
	<p>
		By mapping the <strong>Inputs &rarr; Transformation &rarr; Outputs &rarr; Constraints</strong> of these topics, we diagnose limiting factors and separate factual reality from marketed expectations.
	</p>

	<h2>The Universal Systems Analysis Framework</h2>
	<p>
		Every analysis published on ErrorLedger adheres to a strict editorial methodology. We do not default to negativity, nor do we blindly accept vendor claims. Our framework guarantees:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Evidence-Based Grading:</strong> Every primary source is graded on a strict hierarchy (from Peer-Reviewed Meta-Analyses down to Anecdotal Reports).</li>
		<li><strong>Separation of Fact and Inference:</strong> We explicitly separate observed data from our own engineering deductions.</li>
		<li><strong>Constraint Evaluation:</strong> We evaluate exactly where a system thrives, where it bottlenecks, and where it structurally fails.</li>
		<li><strong>Revision Triggers:</strong> We document the "Known Unknowns" of every topic and establish strict conditions under which our analysis must be re-audited based on new data.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Editorial Independence</h2>
	<p>
		ErrorLedger operates independently. Our standardized scoring metrics (Technical Soundness, Economic Viability, Scalability, Operational Complexity, and Evidence Quality) are applied uniformly to every subject we audit, ensuring our conclusions are driven by data, not bias.
	</p>
</Layout>